WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
INTRO:
The 2008 financial crisis was devastating, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.760
silver lining is that it shed light onto some of&nbsp;
the biggest frauds and shortfalls of the industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.760
Of course, many of the largest players faced no&nbsp;
consequences, but most mid-sized perpetrators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.720
got obliterated. One of these perpetrators was a&nbsp;
hedge fund named Absolute Capital Management run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.200
by Florian Homm. Up until 2008, Absolute Capital&nbsp;
Management or ACM was one of the fastest growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.640
hedge funds in the world and Florian was regarded&nbsp;
as an investing genius. The fund managed roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.880
$3 billion at their peak and they were crushing&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500 year after year. But in late 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.720
Florian could sense that a storm was coming&nbsp;
and that his facade was about to crumble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.840
So, he stuffed $500,000 into his underwear and&nbsp;
disappeared off the face of the planet. ACM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.360
subsequently crashed 93% by June of 2008 and the&nbsp;
SEC would charge Florian with $200 million worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.800
of investment fraud. Since then, Florian has been&nbsp;
on the FBI’s most wanted list, but to this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.360
the FBI hasn’t been able to serve him justice.&nbsp;
And it’s not like they don’t know where he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.600
either. Everyone knows that he’s living in&nbsp;
Germany and he even does interviews to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.600
day claiming his innocence. But despite this, the&nbsp;
FBI has been able to do nothing. So, here’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.080
story of how Florian Homm stole $200 million and&nbsp;
successfully escaped the FBI’s most wanted list.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.720
FLORIAN HOMM:&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.520
Taking a look back, Florian was born on&nbsp;
October 7, 1959 in a small town near Frankfurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.240
Germany called Oberursel. Growing up, Florian&nbsp;
had a pretty nice childhood as his father was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.040
a rather successful businessman. Florian’s father&nbsp;
had built up a pipe welding and bathroom fittings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.440
installation business that was quite lucrative.&nbsp;
Though Florian was grateful for the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.600
he had no interest in following his father&nbsp;
into the business. To Florian, welding pipes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.320
and installing bathroom fixtures sounded extremely&nbsp;
boring. Something that did interest him though was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.080
his great uncle Josef Neckermann. Josef was not&nbsp;
only filthy rich, but he was a superstar. Josef&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.120
was an equestrian olympic athlete who had won 2&nbsp;
gold medals, 2 silver medals, and 2 bronze medals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.640
He also won two world championships and 4 European&nbsp;
championships. And on top of all this, Josef&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.720
started Germany’s largest mail order business&nbsp;
at the time and employed 30,000 employees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.480
So, if Josef were alive today, he would basically&nbsp;
be a billionaire olympian. Josef hosted a family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.640
gathering at his mansion every Sunday, and Florian&nbsp;
recalls that these visits are what convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.880
Florian that he had to be rich just like his&nbsp;
great uncle. And with that in mind, Florian would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.200
be sent off to the US where he attended Bentley&nbsp;
High School in Michigan. Florian ended up crushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.600
high school and would be accepted into Harvard&nbsp;
university where he got a bachelors and an MBA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.920
And following his studies, Florian scored a job&nbsp;
at the prestigious Merrill Lynch. He worked here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.920
for a couple of years, but he would eventually&nbsp;
switch over to Fidelity Investments where he got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.320
the opportunity to work under and be personally&nbsp;
trained by Peter Lynch. After leveraging this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.400
phenomenal opportunity, Florian moved back to&nbsp;
Germany where he would become a senior vice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.840
president at Julius Bar. I think most of us would&nbsp;
agree that Florian was already killing it, but his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.720
ambitions were much larger, so Florian would&nbsp;
go ahead and start his first company in 1993.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.720
GOOD TIMES:&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.400
Florian’s first company was called Value&nbsp;
Management &amp; Research and it was based in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.120
Frankfurt Germany. At first glance, the company&nbsp;
is just another run of the mill hedge fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.560
but Florian’s charm is what would&nbsp;
really attract investors. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.720
Florian had a very peculiar investing philosophy.&nbsp;
He believed that the traditional hedge funds were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.520
doing it all wrong with their fancy algorithms and&nbsp;
complex mathematics. Florian believed that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.000
much better to approach investing holistically.&nbsp;
For example, instead of basing your investments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.840
on quarterly reports, with holistic investing,&nbsp;
you would base your investments on the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.040
and influence of the company. This isn’t exactly&nbsp;
a new idea as long term investors often tend to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.560
more holistic anyway, but for a hedge fund, this&nbsp;
was definitely out of the ordinary. Florian chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.480
a couple of internet and technology hype stocks&nbsp;
to invest in, and thanks to the dotcom bubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.360
these stocks would explode. Florian became iconic&nbsp;
for these investments and these stocks were often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.160
referred to as Homm stocks. Moving past the dotcom&nbsp;
bubble, Florian began to spend his new found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.280
fortune. In 2004, for instance, he invested 20&nbsp;
million euros into the German soccer club Borussia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.840
Dortmund. The soccer team was nearly bankrupt&nbsp;
and was suffering from terrible management.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.920
So, when Florian invested 20 million and overthrew&nbsp;
the old management, he was hailed as a hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.960
by many soccer fans. Florian would leverage this&nbsp;
media attention to start a new hedge fund in 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.520
and that brings us to the infamous Absolute&nbsp;
Capital Management. ACM was launched to great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.680
fanfare and given Florian’s popularity,&nbsp;
charm, and holistic investment strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.200
investors flooded in. ACM did have quite a few&nbsp;
winning picks, but they were all quite odd.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.400
Florian wasn’t investing into Apple or Amazon.&nbsp;
One of his most famous investments was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.200
a Berlin brothel called Artemis. Such weird&nbsp;
choices simply garnered ACM even more attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.960
and Alternative Investment News would even name&nbsp;
ACM as the hedge fund leader of the year. By 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.840
ACM had grown to $3 billion in assets, and&nbsp;
Florian’s personal fortune was worth $700 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.840
But it wasn’t all good as Florian’s great&nbsp;
wealth also attracted negative attention.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.320
FAILED ASSASSINATION:
One day in 2006, as Florian was traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.080
through the streets of Caracas, Venezuela,&nbsp;
he would be stopped by a group of armed men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.080
The armed men demanded everything Florian had&nbsp;
on him which included his wallet, mobile phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.560
and watch. Florian quickly handed over his&nbsp;
wallet and phone, but he didn’t want to part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.960
with his watch. When Florian tried negotiating&nbsp;
to keep his watch though, the armed men started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.840
shooting. Florian was shot in his chest and ended&nbsp;
up losing most of his left lung and his spleen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.800
But thanks to emergency surgery, Florian was able&nbsp;
to survive. While it seems like this was simply a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.520
robbery that went bad, Florian is convinced that&nbsp;
this was an assassination attempt, and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.920
is good reason to believe this. First of all,&nbsp;
Florian wasn’t traveling alone, he was traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.800
with his bodyguard and driver. Not only did the&nbsp;
bodyguard do a poor job at protecting Florian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.840
but he was shot in a much more non lethal way.&nbsp;
The bodyguard was shot in the knee and the driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.400
wasn’t shot at all. So, it’s possible that the&nbsp;
bodyguard and/or driver were working with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.440
robbers. Something else to consider is that, in&nbsp;
most cases, robbers aren’t looking to hurt you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.840
They simply want your money, and being wanted&nbsp;
for homicide is much more risky than being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.640
wanted for a robbery. So, it’s odd that the&nbsp;
robbers were so willing to open fire, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.280
in a lethal way. Despite the sketchiness of&nbsp;
the situation, it was never determined if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.720
really was a failed assasination attempt or simply&nbsp;
idiot robbers. This experience did, however, lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:19.200
to Florian reevaluating his life choices. Florian&nbsp;
says that he had all the money he could ever need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.240
but he was wasting his life working 16 hours a day&nbsp;
6 days a week. He could afford all the luxuries he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.600
wanted, but he didn’t have the time to actually&nbsp;
enjoy them. So, in September of 2007, Florian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.480
announced that he would be selling his stake in&nbsp;
ACM and retiring. This was a bit of a douchebag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.960
move, as his $40 million stock sale crashed the&nbsp;
stock 75% from $2 billion to $500 million. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.200
after the sale, Florian would spread this money&nbsp;
across 120 bank accounts located in 4 different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.400
continents, and he would flee the US. On his&nbsp;
flight out of the US, Florian carried $500,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.120
his underwear and his accomplice carried another&nbsp;
$700,000. This was no doubt a pretty sketchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.760
way to leave, but many were still sympathetic&nbsp;
towards Florian given his near death experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.320
In reality though, Florian had just gotten away&nbsp;
with one of the biggest frauds in US history.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.000
THE FACADE:&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.040
Despite all of the positive press Florian was&nbsp;
receiving, his entire fund was basically a scam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:18.800
He was literally operating fraud on top of fraud&nbsp;
on top of fraud, and the SEC was catching on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.120
The first form of fraud Florian was running was&nbsp;
the classic penny stock pump and dump. Between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.320
2004 and 2006, Florian had been using his massive&nbsp;
cash reserves to buy up low volume penny stocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.480
and dump them onto uneducated investors. Aside&nbsp;
from running a pump and dump, Florian had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.280
clever way to make even more money on the side.&nbsp;
You see, Florian had set up a secondary company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:43.280
that was responsible for executing trades for ACM,&nbsp;
and this company charged insanely high fees. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.840
not only was Florian making money on the pump and&nbsp;
dump itself, but he was making money executing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.000
trades for the pump and dumps. Not all of&nbsp;
ACM’s investments were in pump and dumps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.880
but even the non pump and dumps had fraud&nbsp;
written all over them. For example, Florian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.480
and his partners would regularly trade stocks&nbsp;
amongst themselves to inflate the daily volume of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.400
these stocks and make it seem like these stocks&nbsp;
were way more popular than they really were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.960
ACM also had investments in private companies and&nbsp;
given that these shares weren’t traded publicly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.960
it was ACM’s job to provide their investors with&nbsp;
a fair valuation for these private companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.600
But as you would guess, the valuations that&nbsp;
ACM told their investors were way inflated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.600
Looking back, there were a bunch of red flags&nbsp;
surrounding ACM from the very beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.840
ACM was posting insane growth numbers during a&nbsp;
relatively stagnant time following the dotcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:31.920
crash. Not to mention, ACM was registered in the&nbsp;
Cayman islands. Anyway, the SEC did eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.640
catch on and the FBI would file criminal&nbsp;
charges against Florian in March of 2013.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.280
But, Florian had already lived a free life for 6&nbsp;
years and he had no intention of changing that.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.880
ESCAPING THE FBI:&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.520
Florian wasn’t able to just walk away from the FBI&nbsp;
though. Just two days after the FBI filed criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.400
charges, they were able to locate Florian in Italy&nbsp;
and arrest him. From the very beginning, it seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.640
like Florian's plan was to just battle against&nbsp;
extradition. His lawyers argued that Florian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.120
suffered from multiple sclerosis and that he&nbsp;
couldn’t be extradited due to health concerns. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.680
FBI played along and fought back against Florian’s&nbsp;
lawyers with doctors that testified that Florian’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.880
condition did not prevent incarceration. It seemed&nbsp;
like the FBI was winning as the court would side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.240
with the FBI and agree to extradite Florian which&nbsp;
meant up to a 225 year long prison sentence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.920
While the FBI was celebrating, Florian had&nbsp;
a completely different plan to get out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.720
You see, Italy actually has a rule that once an&nbsp;
extradition decision becomes final, the prisoner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.760
must be extradited within 45 days or they’re&nbsp;
released from prison. It’s not clear if stalling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:37.440
was a 200 IQ move from Florian or if it was&nbsp;
simply an oversight from the FBI. But either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.480
Florian would be released from prison on June 23,&nbsp;
2014. As soon as Florian was released, he bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:46.160
a train ticket to Florence where his lawyers&nbsp;
picked him up and took him to Germany. Florian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.960
crossed the German border the following day,&nbsp;
and this officially marked his freedom. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.440
Germany has a strict policy of not extraditing&nbsp;
German citizens, and since the German courts don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.600
have any arrest warrants against Florian, Florian&nbsp;
is a free man as long as he stays in Germany.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.200
LIFE AS A FREE-ISH MAN:&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.520
Florian is still on the FBI’s most wanted&nbsp;
list, but the FBI has avoided extraditing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.640
him by force because they don’t&nbsp;
want to ruin relations with Germany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.680
In the meantime, Florian has become a completely&nbsp;
different man. He says that his year in Italian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.240
prison completely changed his outlook on life.&nbsp;
He’s transparent that what he did at ACM was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:26.240
completely immoral and unethical, but he still&nbsp;
holds that he didn’t break any laws. Florian has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.920
become a strong christian and spends most of his&nbsp;
time sharing his knowledge and donating his money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.200
For example, he published a book called Rogue&nbsp;
Financier, and he donated 100% of the profits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.520
to the Liberia Renaissance Foundation. He also&nbsp;
co-founded a charity called Our Lady’s Message of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.680
Mercy to the World Foundation. He even has his own&nbsp;
YouTube channel where he discusses his thoughts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:48.480
the world, the economy, and the stock market on a&nbsp;
regular basis. So, Florian has turned into more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.040
a Wolf of Wall Street type figure as opposed to&nbsp;
a Bernie Madoff type figure. Still, some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.600
argue that Florian should be extradited to the US&nbsp;
and jailed for his crimes while others argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.480
what Florian is doing now is a much better way&nbsp;
to repay society instead of just rotting in jail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.360
Which camp are you in? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you’re glad that Florian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.520
is at least changing his ways. And of course,&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

